WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:34.250 --> 00:00:36.320
Hello, my name is Zisl Slepovitch

00:00:36.620 --> 00:00:38.433
and I'm musician-in-residence at Yale

00:00:38.750 --> 00:00:40.710
University's Fortunoff Video Archive for

00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:42.560
Holocaust Testimonies.

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:45.619
This video series is
designed as a companion

00:00:45.740 --> 00:00:47.780
to our album Where Is Our Homeland,

00:00:48.110 --> 00:00:50.299
which is the first volume in the archive's

00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:51.819
ongoing effort to present songs

00:00:52.670 --> 00:00:54.973
and poems performed
by survivors in testimonies

00:00:55.700 --> 00:00:58.069
given to the Fortunoff Archive over

00:00:58.070 --> 00:00:59.500
the last 40 years.

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:01.879
You can learn more about the project on the

00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:05.820
archive's website,
songsfromtestimonies.org.

00:01:06.620 --> 00:01:09.074
Where Is Our Homeland? Songs
from Testimonies, Volume

00:01:09.230 --> 00:01:12.019
1 was recorded in 2019

00:01:12.140 --> 00:01:14.839
by the Zisl Slepovitch Ensemble, featuring

00:01:14.840 --> 00:01:17.340
renowned singer Sasha Lurje,

00:01:17.390 --> 00:01:19.154
Craig Judelman on the violin, Joshua

00:01:19.820 --> 00:01:22.639
Camp on the accordion,
keyboards and guitar,

00:01:22.910 --> 00:01:25.729
Dmitry Ishenko on the bass, and myself

00:01:25.730 --> 00:01:26.730
on woodwinds.

00:01:27.450 --> 00:01:29.655
The songs in this
collection differ vastly in

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:31.573
terms of style, social and historical

00:01:32.130 --> 00:01:35.100
background, language,
and many other aspects,

00:01:35.310 --> 00:01:38.309
but collectively they
create a multidimensional

00:01:38.310 --> 00:01:39.731
picture of Jewish life before

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:42.050
and during World War II.

00:01:44.190 --> 00:01:46.248
We will talk about the Polish Jewish songs

00:01:46.740 --> 00:01:49.409
that were sung by Peretz H., a Holocaust

00:01:49.410 --> 00:01:52.109
survivor born in 1927

00:01:52.110 --> 00:01:53.110
in Warsaw.

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:55.710
When war broke out, he was a little boy.

00:01:56.100 --> 00:01:58.649
Peretz used to sing in
the streets of his home

00:01:58.650 --> 00:02:00.920
city to earn a living and shared

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:03.800
several songs in his 1993

00:02:03.980 --> 00:02:06.380
Fortunoff Video Archive testimony.

00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:09.800
Songs of the survivors, like Peretz,

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:11.421
are of truly special value as

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:15.069
they shed a unique light on the Polish

00:02:15.070 --> 00:02:18.000
and more specifically, Warsaw music culture

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:20.360
of the interwar and World War II

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:22.720
periods. Some of the texts, like

00:02:22.840 --> 00:02:25.320
this one in Peretz's testimony, are

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:28.199
not to be found in any known sources

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:29.200
today.

00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:32.519
W pociagu jest tlok, Crowded Train,

00:02:32.730 --> 00:02:35.339
is one of at least two Polish

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:38.280
renditions of the popular Mexican copla

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:40.360
song Cielito Lindo.

00:02:40.600 --> 00:02:43.160
Just like Walc Francois that we discussed

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:46.119
in the first episode, this is the song

00:02:46.120 --> 00:02:47.120
that Peretz H.

00:02:47.260 --> 00:02:49.450
sang in the courtyards of Warsaw,

00:02:49.690 --> 00:02:52.629
along with many other
children like himself,

00:02:53.170 --> 00:02:55.500
as well as local street bands, which would

00:02:55.540 --> 00:02:58.080
have been lost to history, if not for

00:02:58.280 --> 00:02:59.920
the testimony of Peretz H.

00:03:00.590 --> 00:03:03.320
It refers to the depression and smuggling

00:03:03.350 --> 00:03:06.019
of groceries in the prewar Warsaw.

00:03:06.470 --> 00:03:09.349
In postwar Poland, the song gained

00:03:09.470 --> 00:03:11.720
newfound popularity, thanks in

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:14.400
part to Leonard Buczkowsky's

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:17.240
1946 movie, Zakazane

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:19.280
piosenki, Forbidden Songs.

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:21.800
The new version, although similar to

00:03:21.960 --> 00:03:24.560
the prewar version sung by Peretz,

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:26.090
referred to the war.

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:28.840
"Teraz jest wojna, kto handluje

00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:30.920
ten zyje." It is war now.

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:32.959
Those who sell, live.

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:36.360
Decades later in 1982,

00:03:36.520 --> 00:03:39.040
the Polish Cielito Lindo came

00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:41.560
back to life with a new lyrics, yet

00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:44.400
again in the wake of
the military coup d'etat.

